From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 05:00:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] perf(previews): the canvas shows each frame's preview at
 the scale it is on screen

A frame without a live document shows a picture of itself. That picture
was the dashboard thumbnail: one size for every frame and every zoom,
min(1, 640 / width), JPEG at quality 70, clipped at 1200 px of height.
On a real canvas (a 2x display):

- a 1440x900 screen at 27% is 786 device px across and was drawn from
  640, stretched, with JPEG ringing round every glyph;
- a 12640x400 section banner (the titles a page is read by at fit zoom)
  got a 640x20 picture, drawn 2360 px wide: unreadable;
- a frame taller than 1200 px showed only its top.

Selecting one of them gives it a live document, crisp among its blurred
neighbours: the same frame looked like two different designs.

Now there is a short pyramid, as for map tiles: the whole frame at 0.25,
0.5 and 1 output px per frame px (PREVIEW_SCALES), WebP at quality 90,
/i/<id>.webp?preview&s=<scale>&v=<version>. Each level is rendered when
first asked for and stored like the thumbnail, with its own version
(thumbs.ts), and a frame too large for a level is rendered as large as
the limits allow (8 megapixels, 16000 px a side).

The canvas asks for the smallest level that covers a frame at the
resting camera's zoom x devicePixelRatio, so a picture is scaled down
and never stretched, and only for the frames in or near the view; the
rest of the page keeps the 0.25 picture. (Without that limit a zoom-in
left every far preview with the large picture queued, and a jump back to
the whole page loaded hundreds of them at once.) A level change keeps
the picture on screen until the sharper one has loaded. A page that has
just opened asks for nothing until its camera has rested once, since its
zoom until then is the previous page's.

Stored pictures, 219 frames of that canvas: 0.25 median 4.3 KB, 0.5
8.4 KB, 1 33.7 KB; the 640 px JPEG was 11.7 KB. So the whole-page view,
where every frame is a picture, now moves a third of the bytes and
decodes a third of the pixels (360x225 against 640x400 per screen).
Editor main thread on the 188-frame page, interleaved with the previous
build on a loaded machine, median of 4: pan at fit 654 -> 596 ms, zoom
8% -> 60% 850 -> 1066 ms, pan at 60% 1427 -> 1495 ms, fit jump
305 -> 195 ms; the spread between runs of one build is larger than any
of these differences.

